WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.266
In an earlier domain, we introduced the concept of private IP addresses and their use in the overall IP addressing scheme.

00:07.266 --> 00:14.118
And, since this particular topic is mentioned several times in this course, we will recap those address numbers later.

00:14.118 --> 00:27.880
Two more ranges of IP addresses of note are multicast addresses and reserved addresses. Take a look at this routing table. In particular, I want you to notice this 224.0.0.0 address.

00:27.880 --> 00:37.693
This is the first address in the range of multicast addresses. Multicast addresses are not assigned to devices.

00:37.693 --> 00:43.670
They are used when one device needs to send the same data to multiple devices at once.

00:43.670 --> 00:53.380
The IP address range for multicast IP addresses is any IP address starting with the numbers 224 through 239.

00:53.380 --> 00:59.438
Not seen here is a big range of IP addresses with numbers starting from 240 to 255.

00:59.438 --> 01:10.281
Those are used for experimental purposes, except for this address of 255.255.255.255, which is the overall broadcast address.

01:10.281 --> 01:21.182
Data sent to this address is sent to every device on a network. This is done when, for example, a device needs to know the MAC address of a device with a certain IP address.

01:21.182 --> 01:31.744
It asks, quote unquote, everyone on the network for a match. That is the purpose of the overall broadcast address.

